Add Read: one domain by name, answered with an Actual from the accepted ontology

Dev accepted Read's second design point on 2026-10-13 ("I accept Read naming one domain
answered with an Actual, proceed"). Two facts bounded it: definitions are keyed by name and
package, parameters free, so a domain is named the same way; and the ontology has no
codec, so no answer can carry the ontology itself — a user reads it one domain at a time,
which is what the Editor can honestly promise.

draco.dreams.user.Read derives Request and carries one element, `fix domain draco.TypeName`;
User holds Latent, Read, Request and Response. The Editor's message body gains its second
branch, `case read draco.dreams.user.Read [ ... ]`: `fix owner Presence(DomainType)
presence(accepted.keys.find(\d -> namePath == read.domain.namePath))`, then
`actual(owner.fold(<absent>, \d -> Actual(d.typeDefinition, d.typeDictionary.elementTypes)))`.
When the accepted ontology owns the domain the answer is an Actual of its definition and
members as the ontology holds them, no problems; when it owns none the answer is an Actual
with a stub definition naming the domain, no members and one Problem ("domain X is not in
the accepted ontology"), so `accepted` is false and the user reads it as any other
Response. A Read never touches the state and never delivers an ontology; the two
consecutive cases are one dispatch (drake.dlt CASE-BRANCH). Nothing was added to the
Editor's factory or to User's response vocabulary. In Haskell: Read is the constructor
applied to a name, and the answer is the same Response the other constructors yield.

